# Remote artifacts a Dockerfile installs WITHOUT cryptographic verification.
#
# Read by scripts/check-unverified-downloads.ts. An entry is a substring of the
# URL (or the image ref) that identifies the fetch, e.g. `awscli.amazonaws.com`.
#
# THE BAR IS "no verification is POSSIBLE", not "no verification is convenient".
# If upstream publishes a sha256, a checksums.txt, or a release-API digest, pin it
# instead of adding a line here -- five downloads were fixed that way on
# 2026-09-01 and only these needed an exemption.
#
# Each entry needs a substantive BLOCKER reason (>=30 chars, no banned phrases),
# validated by scripts/lib/blocker-validator.ts, and its liveness is probed by
# scripts/check-suppression-liveness.ts.

# BLOCKER: AWS publishes no sha256 for the CLI bundle -- the .sha256 URL returns 404 -- only a GPG .sig, which needs their signing key imported first; revisit if a checksum ever appears
awscli.amazonaws.com

# BLOCKER: piped straight into bash, so there is no artifact on disk to hash; only vendoring the installer script into this repo would make it verifiable
raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm

# BLOCKER: piped straight into bash, same shape as the nvm installer above; the vendor publishes no pinned, checksummed release artifact to use instead
claude.ai/install.sh

# BLOCKER: this is an apt KEYRING, and apt itself verifies every package installed from that repo against it via signed-by, so the packages are covered even though this fetch is not
download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg
